'''Bradley Jay Little''' (born February 15, 1954)<ref>{{cite news|url = https://www.newspapers.com/image/395313897/|title = Little replaces Risch as Lt. Gov.|last = Miller|first = John|newspaper = [[Times-News (Idaho)|The Times-News]]|date = January 7, 2009|agency = [[Associated Press]]|page = A1|via = [[Newspapers.com]]|url-access = subscription}}</ref> is an American politician serving since 2019 as the 33rd [[governor of Idaho]]. A member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]], he served as the 42nd [[lieutenant governor of Idaho]] from 2009 to 2019 and as an [[Idaho Senate|Idaho state senator]] from 2001 to 2009.

Little is a graduate of the [[University of Idaho]], having earned a [[Bachelor of Science]] degree in 1976. Little ran his family's ranching operation and served on various business and civic boards, including 20 years with the Idaho Association of Commerce and Industry, before being appointed Idaho state senator by Governor [[Dirk Kempthorne]] in 2001.<ref name="LGO of Idaho 2018">{{cite web |title=Idaho's Lieutenant Governor |website=lgo.idaho.gov |date=2018-05-14 |url=https://lgo.idaho.gov/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180514134323/https://lgo.idaho.gov/ |archive-date=2018-05-14 |url-status=live |access-date=2018-07-09}}</ref> In 2009, Governor [[Butch Otter]] appointed Little as lieutenant governor after [[Jim Risch]] resigned to become a [[United States Senate|United States senator]].

Little was elected governor in [[2018 Idaho gubernatorial election|2018]] after winning a crowded Republican primary with 37.3% of the vote.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2018/05/15/us/elections/results-idaho-primary-elections.html,%20https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2018/05/15/us/elections/results-idaho-primary-elections.html|title=Idaho Primary Election Results|last=Almukhtar|first=Sarah|date=2018-05-15|work=The New York Times|access-date=2018-05-22|language=en-US|issn=0362-4331}}</ref> He won renomination with 52.8% of the vote, and was reelected in [[2022 Idaho gubernatorial election|2022]], defeating Democratic nominee Stephen Heidt and [[Independent (United States)|independent]] candidate [[Ammon Bundy]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=November 8, 2022 |title=Republican Brad Little wins reelection for governor in Idaho |url=https://www.sandiegouniontribune.com/news/nation-world/story/2022-11-08/republican-brad-little-wins-reelection-for-governor-in-idaho |website=[[The San Diego Union-Tribune]]}}</ref>

== Early life and education ==
Little was born and raised in [[Emmett, Idaho]] and graduated from [[Emmett High School]] in 1972.<ref name="auto">{{cite book|last1=Shadduck|first1=Louise|title=Andy Little: Idaho Sheep King|date=1990|publisher=Caxton Printers, Ltd.|isbn=0-87004-340-4|location=Caldwell, Idaho}}</ref> He attended the [[University of Idaho]] in {{nowrap|[[Moscow, Idaho|Moscow]],<ref name=gem73stu>{{cite web |url=https://issuu.com/uidahodigital/docs/gem1973/156 |publisher=Gem of the Mountains, University of Idaho yearbook |title=Students |year=1973 |page=152}}</ref>}} was a member of the Idaho Alpha chapter of {{nowrap|[[Phi Delta Theta]]}} {{nowrap|[[Fraternities and sororities|fraternity]],<ref name=gem73pdth>{{cite web|url=https://issuu.com/uidahodigital/docs/gem1973/238|publisher=Gem of the Mountains, University of Idaho yearbook |title=Phi Delta Theta |year=1973 |page=234}}</ref><ref name=gem74pdth>{{cite web|url=https://issuu.com/uidahodigital/docs/gem1974/248|publisher=Gem of the Mountains, University of Idaho yearbook |title=Phi Delta Theta |year=1974 |page=245}}</ref>}} and earned a [[B.S.]] in [[agribusiness]] in 1977.<ref>{{cite web|title=About Governor Brad Little|url=https://gov.idaho.gov/our-governor/|website=Idaho.gov}}</ref>

== Career ==
[[File:Littles with Rischs - 1-7-11 (15519104474).jpg|thumb|left|Little at his 2011 inauguration, with U.S. Senator [[Jim Risch]] and their wives]]
Little has had an extensive dual career tending to his family's ranching interests (his grandfather was the {{nowrap|"Idaho Sheep King")<ref name="auto"/>}} and in public service. During the 1981 and 1985 legislative sessions, Little represented his father, David Little, in the Senate on a temporary appointment due to illness, during which time he served on the Finance and Resources Committees.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?nid=1314&dat=19860206&id=KllWAAAAIBAJ&pg=7134,3595640|title=The Spokesman-Review - Google News Archive Search|website=news.google.com|access-date=1 October 2018}}</ref> Little also managed his family's ranching operation, Little Land and Livestock, for almost 30 years until his son, David, became manager in 2009 when Little was appointed lieutenant governor.<ref name="bradlittleforidaho1">Brad Little for Idaho, {{cite web|title=Meet Brad Little |url=https://www.bradlittleforidaho.com/meet-brad-little/|access-date=2021-08-10}}</ref> He continues to work as the head of Little Enterprises, Inc. (a diversified farming and cattle operation), and is a member of the board of directors of Performance Design Inc., a small Boise-based manufacturing company.<ref name="bradlittleforidaho1" />

Little has also been involved in a variety of private organizations and companies based in Idaho and the Mountain West. He is a former chairman of the Idaho Association of Commerce and Industry (IACI), "The Voice of Business in Idaho", and was a member of its board for 20 years (1981–2001).<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.iaci.org/Portals/0/Files/2009%20Conference%20Report%20Summary.pdf |title=Archived copy |access-date=2014-07-30 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140810052608/http://www.iaci.org/Portals/0/Files/2009%20Conference%20Report%20Summary.pdf |archive-date=2014-08-10 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Little is also the former vice-chairman of the Idaho Community Foundation and the Emmett Public School Foundation, and the former director of the Idaho Wool Growers Association and the University of Idaho Foundation.<ref name="bradlittleforidaho1"/><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.idcomfdn.org/historical-board |title=Historical Board - Idaho Community Foundation |access-date=2014-07-30 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140815041618/http://www.idcomfdn.org/historical-board |archive-date=2014-08-15 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.emmettschools.org/domain/74|title=Emmett Public School Foundation / Emmett Public School Foundation|website=Emmettschools.org|access-date=1 October 2018}}</ref> He has also served in the past on the boards of directors of [[High Country News]], Home Federal Bank, a small Idaho-based regional bank recently acquired by Bank of the Cascades, and the Idaho Foundation for Excellence in Education.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/apps/news?pid=newsarchive&sid=a_fuLqMmtXCg|title=Terms of Service Violation|website=Bloomberg.com|access-date=1 October 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hcn.org/blogs/goat/former-hcn-board-member-named-idaho-lieutenant|title=Ex-HCN board member named Idaho lt. guv|website=Hcn.org|date=6 January 2009|access-date=1 October 2018}}</ref>

=== State senator (2001–2009) ===
[[Dirk Kempthorne|Governor Dirk Kempthorne]] appointed Little to fill a state Senate vacancy in May 2001. He represented what was at the time District 8, which covered a part of <!--Little's native-->[[Gem County, Idaho|Gem County]] surrounding and north of Emmett, all of [[Boise County, Idaho|Boise]], [[Valley County, Idaho|Valley]], and [[Adams County, Idaho|Adams]] Counties, and the southern portion of [[Idaho County, Idaho|Idaho County]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.legislature.idaho.gov/sessioninfo/2002/02sjournal.pdf |title=Archived copy |access-date=2014-07-30 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304115535/http://www.legislature.idaho.gov/sessioninfo/2002/02sjournal.pdf |archive-date=2016-03-04 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.legislature.idaho.gov/redistricting/archive/maps/L1990_B_State.pdf |title=Archived copy |access-date=2014-07-30 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304124559/http://www.legislature.idaho.gov/redistricting/archive/maps/L1990_B_State.pdf |archive-date=2016-03-04 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

After a change in district boundaries due to redistricting in 2001–02, Little was elected in the fall of 2002 to District 11, which then encompassed all of [[Gem County, Idaho|Gem County]] and the northern portion of [[Canyon County, Idaho|Canyon County]], including the communities of [[Middleton, Idaho|Middleton]] and [[Parma, Idaho|Parma]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.legislature.idaho.gov/sessioninfo/2003/journals/sfinal.pdf |title=Archived copy |access-date=2014-07-30 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304191909/http://www.legislature.idaho.gov/sessioninfo/2003/journals/sfinal.pdf |archive-date=2016-03-04 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.legislature.idaho.gov/redistricting/archive/maps/L97_E_State.pdf |title=Archived copy |access-date=2014-07-30 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304124514/http://www.legislature.idaho.gov/redistricting/archive/maps/L97_E_State.pdf |archive-date=2016-03-04 |url-status=dead }}</ref> He was reelected senator from the 11th legislative district four times.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.sos.idaho.gov/elect/results/2004/general/tot_leg.htm|title=2004 General Results legislative|website=Sos.idaho.gov|access-date=2017-05-03|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120501062423/http://www.sos.idaho.gov/ELECT/RESULTS/2004/general/tot_leg.htm|archive-date=2012-05-01}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.sos.idaho.gov/elect/results/2006/general/tot_leg.htm|title=2006 General Results legislative|website=Sos.idaho.gov|access-date=2017-05-03|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120501064151/http://www.sos.idaho.gov/elect/RESULTS/2006/general/tot_leg.htm|archive-date=2012-05-01}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.sos.idaho.gov/elect/results/2008/General/tot_leg.htm|title=2008 General Results legislative|website=Sos.idaho.gov|access-date=2017-05-03|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120616143914/http://www.sos.idaho.gov/elect/RESULTS/2008/General/tot_leg.htm|archive-date=2012-06-16}}</ref> Little was also elected in 2003 by his [[Republican Party of Idaho|Republican]] peers to the party leadership position of Majority Caucus Chair, which he held until 2009.<ref>Brad Little for Idaho, {{cite web|url=http://bradlittleforidaho.com/about-brad/|title=About Brad - Brad Little for Idaho - Campaign 2014|access-date=2014-07-30|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140628181344/http://bradlittleforidaho.com/about-brad/|archive-date=2014-06-28}}</ref>

