=== Lieutenant governor of Idaho (2009–2019) ===
[[File:Little presiding over State Senate - 12-1-11 (15955758337).jpg|thumb|Little presiding over the Idaho Senate in 2011]]

==== Appointment, election and reelection ====
In January 2009, Governor [[Butch Otter]] appointed Little to the office of [[lieutenant governor]] to fill the vacancy left by former Lieutenant Governor [[Jim Risch]]'s election to the [[United States Senate|U.S. Senate]] in 2008. Little was sworn in by Otter on January 6, 2009, and confirmed by unanimous consent when the Idaho Senate convened on January 12.<ref>KBOI 2, {{cite web|url=http://www.kboi2.com/news/37150039.html |title=Brad Little named Lieutenant Governor &#124; News &#124; Boise, Meridian, Nampa, Caldwell, Idaho News, Weather, Sports and Breaking News - KBOI 2 |access-date=2014-08-06 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150224105943/http://www.kboi2.com/news/37150039.html |archive-date=2015-02-24 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://legislature.idaho.gov/sessioninfo/2009/journals/sfinal.pdf |title=Archived copy |access-date=2014-08-06 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150905162706/http://legislature.idaho.gov/sessioninfo/2009/journals/sfinal.pdf |archive-date=2015-09-05 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Little was elected lieutenant governor in 2010, defeating two opponents in the primary election and two opponents from the Democratic and Constitution parties in the general election.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.sos.idaho.gov/elect/RESULTS/2010/Primary/tot_stwd.htm |title=2010 Primary Results statewide |access-date=2014-08-06 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130707164540/http://www.sos.idaho.gov/elect/RESULTS/2010/Primary/tot_stwd.htm |archive-date=2013-07-07 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.spokesman.com/elections/2010/idaho-general-election/races/statewide/idaho/lieutenant-governor/|title=Election Center|website=Spokesman.com|access-date=1 October 2018}}</ref> He was reelected in [[2014 Idaho elections#Lieutenant governor|2014]].

