==== Economic development and trade missions ====
Little focused on economic development as lieutenant governor, helping persuade energy bar producer [[Clif Bar]] to build a new food manufacturing plant in Idaho in 2013.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.idahostatesman.com/2014/05/02/3164594/candidates-for-lt-gov-differ-on.html |title=Candidates for lieutenant governor differ on role &#124; State Elections &#124; Idahostatesman.com |website=www.idahostatesman.com |access-date=26 January 2022 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20140814182752/http://www.idahostatesman.com/2014/05/02/3164594/candidates-for-lt-gov-differ-on.html |archive-date=14 August 2014 |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Idaho Officials Move at the Speed of Business|url=http://bxjmag.com/idaho-officials-move-at-the-speed-of-business/|access-date=1 October 2018|website=Bxjmag.com}}</ref>

Little also took part in and led several trade missions. He led a Friendship Mission to the [[Basque Country (autonomous community)|Basque Country]] in Spain in 2010, during which he met the President of the Basque Government [[Patxi López]]. During this meeting, Little and López agreed to establish a Basque Economic Development Office in Boise that "would provide resources and services for Idaho and Basque companies to ease collaboration on research, sales and collaborative programs."<ref>{{cite web|url=http://idahoreporter.com/little-comes-back-from-basque-visit-with-several-agreements/|title=Home - Idaho Freedom Foundation|website=idahoreporter.com|access-date=1 October 2018|archive-date=24 February 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150224094852/http://idahoreporter.com/little-comes-back-from-basque-visit-with-several-agreements/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Little later signed the Euskadi-Idaho Friendship Agreement, which affirms the friendship and cultural affinity between the Basque Country and Idaho, which has the largest Basque community outside Spain.<ref>Cenarrusa Foundation for Basque Culture, {{cite web|url=https://idahopolicy.wordpress.com/tag/cenarrusa-foundation-for-basque-culture/ |title=Cenarrusa Foundation for Basque Culture &#124; Roy Eiguren, Idaho attorney and lobbyist |access-date=2014-08-14 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140819085513/https://idahopolicy.wordpress.com/tag/cenarrusa-foundation-for-basque-culture/ |archive-date=2014-08-19 }}</ref>

Little was also a member of a 2011 Idaho trade delegation that traveled to [[Mexico]] and [[Brazil]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.opb.org/news/article/idaho_trade_mission_results_in_promising_leads/|title=Idaho Trade Mission Results In Promising Leads|website=Opb.org|access-date=1 October 2018|archive-date=16 November 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181116052012/https://www.opb.org/news/article/idaho_trade_mission_results_in_promising_leads/|url-status=dead}}</ref> After the trade mission, he said, "we found tremendous interest and opportunities in both countries for Idaho products and services … This trip strengthened key trade relationships and established new customers for Idaho businesses." The [[Idaho Department of Commerce]] estimated that the mission resulted in sales of more than $30 million.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://lgo.idaho.gov/pdf/12-15-2011,%20Idaho%20ag%20leaders%20believe%20trade%20mission%20a%20success.pdf |title=Archived copy |access-date=2014-08-14 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120502015557/http://lgo.idaho.gov/pdf/12-15-2011,%20Idaho%20ag%20leaders%20believe%20trade%20mission%20a%20success.pdf |archive-date=2012-05-02 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

==== Legislation ====
In the 2014 legislative session, Little sponsored Senate Bill 1354, an anti-"[[patent troll]]" bill. The bill protects companies from abusive or "bad faith assertions of [[patent infringement]]" to collect an extortionate licensing fee.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.legislature.idaho.gov/legislation/2014/S1354.pdf |title=Archived copy |access-date=2014-08-14 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140819085837/http://www.legislature.idaho.gov/legislation/2014/S1354.pdf |archive-date=2014-08-19 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://idahobusinessreview.com/2014/03/21/patent-troll-bill-will-protect-idaho-businesses/|title='Patent Troll' bill will protect Idaho businesses|first1=Amy|last1=Lombardo|first2=Brad|last2=Little|date=21 March 2014|website=Idahobusinessreview.com|access-date=1 October 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://lgo.idaho.gov/pdf/Lt%20%20Gov%20Little%20Press%20Release-%203-18-14.pdf |title=Archived copy |access-date=2014-08-14 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140708002216/http://lgo.idaho.gov/pdf/Lt%20%20Gov%20Little%20Press%20Release-%203-18-14.pdf |archive-date=2014-07-08 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

===Governor of Idaho (2019–present)===
====2018 election====
In June 2016, Little announced his candidacy for the [[2018 Idaho gubernatorial election|Idaho gubernatorial election in 2018]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.idahostatesman.com/news/politics-government/state-politics/article86578672.html|title=Brad Little, Idaho's governor-in-waiting, commits to 2018 run|work=idahostatesman|access-date=2017-04-10|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.idahostatesman.com/news/politics-government/politics-columns-blogs/politics-blog/article129916114.html|title=Lt.Gov. Brad Little raises $340,000 for Idaho governor's race in 2018|work=idahostatesman|access-date=2017-04-10|language=en}}</ref> He said that [[Idaho National Laboratory]] would be a priority if he became governor.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.postregister.com/articles/news-daily-email-todays-headlines/2017/04/22/little-says-inl-would-be-priority|title=Little says INL would be priority|date=2017-04-22|work=Post Register|access-date=2017-04-24|language=en}}</ref>

Little was endorsed by incumbent Governor Otter,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://idahostatejournal.com/community/governor-c-l-butch-otter-officially-endorses-brad-little-for/article_0412dde1-039b-5e9f-b6bb-38a4f804a8d4.html |title=Gov. Otter officially endorses Brad Little for governor &#124; Community |website=Idahostatejournal.com |date=2018-04-10 |access-date=2018-05-14}}</ref> former governors [[Dirk Kempthorne]] and [[Phil Batt]], and U.S. Senator Jim Risch.<ref>{{cite web|author=Category: politics |url=http://idahopoliticsweekly.com/politics/2080-little-gubernatorial-campaign-announces-endorsements/ |title=Little gubernatorial campaign announces endorsements |website=Idahopoliticsweekly.com |date=2018-05-06 |access-date=2018-05-14}}</ref>
[[File:President Donald J. Trump Meets with Governors-Elect (32440546418).jpg|thumb|300x300px|Little speaks during a meeting at the White House with [[Donald Trump|President Trump]] and [[Mike Pence|Vice President Pence]] and fellow governors-elect.]]
During his campaign, Little called for a phased-in $350 million reduction in the state income tax and the elimination of the Idaho grocery tax.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.spokesman.com/blogs/boise/2017/dec/06/little-taxes-ive-got-concrete-plan-because-ive-been-here-ive-listened/|title=Little on taxes: 'I've got a concrete plan because I've been here, I've listened'|website=Spokesman.com|access-date=1 October 2018}}</ref>

Little won the [[Idaho Republican Party]] primary, beating both U.S. Representative [[Raúl Labrador]] and businessman Tommy Ahlquist with 37.3% of the vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://sos.idaho.gov/elect/results/2018/Primary/statewide_totals.html|title=Statewide Totals|website=sos.idaho.gov|access-date=2018-06-19}}</ref> In the general election in November, he defeated state Representative [[Paulette Jordan]], the [[Idaho Democratic Party]] nominee,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://sos.idaho.gov/elect/candidat/2018/CandidateList_General.html|title=Report Declaration|website=sos.idaho.gov|access-date=2018-06-19}}</ref> by over 130,000 votes.

==== 2022 reelection ====
In March 2022, Little filed papers to run for a second term in office, having announced his intention to run the previous month.<ref>{{Cite web |date=February 16, 2022 |title=Idaho Gov. Brad Little running for re-election |url=https://www.ktvb.com/video/news/local/208/idaho-gov-brad-little-running-for-re-election/277-dedb0356-0fca-40cb-a309-e94b81cf0bec |website=KTVB |access-date=December 11, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=March 12, 2022 |title=Idaho Gov. Brad Little files for re-election, enters GOP governor's primary |url=https://www.spokesman.com/stories/2022/mar/12/idaho-gov-brad-little-files-for-re-election-enters/ |website=[[The Spokesman-Review]] |last=Corbin |first=Clark |access-date=December 11, 2022}}</ref> He won the Republican nomination in May, defeating Lieutenant Governor [[Janice McGeachin]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Idaho Election Results 2022 {{!}} Live Primary Map Updates |url=https://www.politico.com/2022-election/results/results/idaho/ |access-date=2022-10-05 |website=www.politico.com |language=en }}{{Dead link|date=October 2023 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>

The Democratic nominee in the race was Stephen Heidt. At the same time, an anti-government activist, [[Ammon Bundy]], ran in the race as an independent.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Colley|first1=Bill|title=Ammon Bundy to Officially Announce for Idaho Governor |url=https://newsradio1310.com/ammon-bundy-to-officially-announce-for-idaho-governor|date=June 10, 2021|access-date=June 10, 2021|website=[[KLIX (AM)]]}}</ref> Little easily won [[2022 Idaho gubernatorial election|the November 8 election]], certifying the win in every county except [[Blaine County, Idaho|Blaine County]], which Heidt won.<ref>{{Cite web |date=November 8, 2022 |title=Idaho Republican Gov. Brad Little easily wins second term |url=https://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2022/nov/8/gov-brad-little-easily-wins-second-term-in-idaho/ |website=KTVB |access-date=December 11, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=November 10, 2022 |title=Idaho Gov. Brad Little wins second term against independent, Democratic challengers |url=https://www.idahostatesman.com/news/politics-government/state-politics/article268155082.html |website=[[Idaho Statesman]] |access-date=December 11, 2022}}</ref>

==== Tenure ====
In March 2020, Little gained attention for signing two bills into law that addressed [[transgender rights|transgender people]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.hrc.org/news/amid-global-pandemic-idaho-governor-brad-little-prioritizes-and-signs-anti|title=Idaho Governor Brad Little Signs Anti-Transgender Legislation|date=30 March 2020 }}</ref> The first bans transgender women and girls from [[transgender people in sports|competing]] in women's sports, citing possible unfair physical advantages. The second bill, HB 509, bans transgender people from changing the sex on their birth certificates.<ref>{{cite web| url = https://www.pinknews.co.uk/2020/05/20/idaho-transgender-birth-certificate-law-hb509-transphobic-lambda-legal/| title = Idaho is actually arguing that its hateful birth certificate law isn't transphobic| date = 20 May 2020}}</ref><ref name="CNNTransBill">{{cite web|last1=Rose|first1=Andy|last2=Silverman|first2=Hollie|title=Idaho Governor Signs Two Bills that Limit the Rights of Transgender People|url=https://www.cnn.com/2020/03/31/us/idaho-transgender-bills/index.html|access-date=11 April 2020|website=CNN.com|date=31 March 2020 }}</ref><ref name="NBCTransBill">{{cite web|title=Idaho Governor Signs Into Law Anti-Transgender Legislation|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/feature/nbc-out/idaho-governor-signs-law-anti-transgender-legislation-n1172886|access-date=11 April 2020|website=NBCNews.com|date=31 March 2020 }}</ref>

In 2021, Little signed legislation that raised signature requirements for ballot initiatives.<ref>{{Cite news|last1=Epstein|first1=Reid J.|last2=Corasaniti|first2=Nick|date=2021-05-22|title=Republicans Move to Limit a Grass-Roots Tradition of Direct Democracy|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/22/us/politics/republican-ballot-initiatives-democrats.html|access-date=2021-05-25|issn=0362-4331}}</ref> That year, he also signed legislation that would permit killing up to 90% of the state's estimated 1,500 wolves to the minimum level of 150 as set by Idaho's wolf conservation and management plan; the legislation was backed by the ranching sector of Idaho, but strongly opposed by environmental advocates.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Bill to kill up to 90% of Idaho wolves signed by governor|url=https://abcnews.go.com/Business/wireStory/bill-kill-90-idaho-wolves-signed-governor-77560502|access-date=2021-05-31|website=ABC News|language=en}}</ref>

== Political positions ==

=== Abortion ===
In late April 2021, Little signed House Bill 366, effectively [[Six-week abortion ban|prohibiting abortions after about six weeks of pregnancy]], making exceptions for victims of rape, incest, and for medical emergencies. He also said, "We should never relent in our efforts to protect the lives of the preborn" and "Hundreds and hundreds of babies lose their lives every year in Idaho due to abortion, an absolute tragedy."<ref>{{Cite web|author=Caroline Kelly|title=Idaho GOP governor signs 'heartbeat' abortion ban into law|url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/04/27/politics/idaho-heartbeat-abortion-ban/index.html|access-date=2021-11-01|website=CNN|date=28 April 2021 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=April 27, 2021|title=Gov. Little signs 'fetal heartbeat' abortion ban bill into law|url=https://www.ktvb.com/article/news/local/capitol-watch/gov-little-signs-fetal-heartbeat-abortion-bill-into-law/277-7e0d0625-4254-4c72-919c-1bd0cf8ede4c|access-date=2021-11-01|website=ktvb.com}}</ref>

In March 2022, Little signed Senate Bill 1309 modeled after the [[Texas Heartbeat Act]] that prohibited abortions after about six weeks of pregnancy. The bill made exceptions for victims of rape, incest, and for medical emergencies.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Moseley-Morris |first=Kelcie |date=2022-03-23 |title=Idaho governor signs bill effectively banning most abortions |url=https://idahocapitalsun.com/2022/03/23/idaho-governor-signs-bill-effectively-banning-most-abortions/ |access-date=2022-04-21 |website=Idaho Capital Sun}}</ref> The Idaho Supreme Court later temporarily blocked the law.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Idaho Supreme Court temporarily blocks new ban on abortions after six weeks |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/nation/2022/04/09/idaho-abortion-law-2022-temporarily-blocked/9525206002/ |access-date=2022-04-21 |website=USA Today}}</ref>

In April 2023, Little signed State House Bill 242, which prohibits "recruiting, harboring, or transporting" minors across state lines for abortions without explicit parental consent. It also makes it illegal to obtain abortion pills for a minor. A conviction for "abortion trafficking" carries a minimum sentence of two years in state prison and a maximum of five years. This is the first anti-abortion bill to prosecute people who travel to states where abortions are legal to undergo the procedure.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Idaho governor signs ban on 'abortion trafficking' |url=https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/idaho-governor-signs-ban-on-abortion-trafficking |access-date=2023-04-29 |website=PBS|date=6 April 2023 }}</ref>

=== Gun control ===
Little opposes [[gun control]]. In May 2021, he signed a bill that would thwart nearly a half-dozen of executive orders from [[Joe Biden|President Joe Biden]] combating gun control.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2021-05-11|title=Idaho governor signs bill to halt Biden moves on gun laws|url=https://apnews.com/article/id-state-wire-idaho-gun-politics-laws-business-e0bd8660c1836de58dcc906c50ac0292|access-date=2021-11-01|website=AP NEWS|language=en-US|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210511212014/https://apnews.com/article/id-state-wire-idaho-gun-politics-laws-business-e0bd8660c1836de58dcc906c50ac0292|archive-date=2021-05-11|url-status=live}}</ref> Little has an A+ rating from the [[NRA Political Victory Fund]] for his record on Second Amendment rights and was endorsed in the 2022 election.<ref>{{cite web |title=NRA-PVF {{!}} Idaho |url=http://nrapvf.org/grades/idaho |website=nrapvf.org |publisher=NRA-PVF |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221108112719/http://nrapvf.org/grades/idaho |archive-date=November 8, 2022 |language=en-US |url-status=usurped}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=April 18, 2022 |title=NRA Political Victory Fund endorses Brad Little |url=https://www.kmvt.com/2022/04/18/nra-political-victory-fund-endorses-brad-little/ |website=KMTV |access-date=December 11, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220418195853/https://www.kmvt.com/2022/04/18/nra-political-victory-fund-endorses-brad-little |archive-date=April 18, 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== LGBT rights ===
In March 2020, Little signed both House Bill 500 and House Bill 509, which ban transgender women from playing on women's athletic teams and prohibit people from changing their gender mark on their birth certificate.<ref>{{Cite web|date=April 8, 2020|title=Gov. Little defends two anti-transgender bills he signed into Idaho law|url=https://www.ktvb.com/article/news/local/capitol-watch/governor-little-defends-transgender-bills/277-c6200fdc-b0b5-47c6-8807-06d18a74a50f|access-date=2021-11-01|website=ktvb.com|language=en-US}}</ref>

In April 2023, Little signed House Bill 71 into law, banning anyone under the age of 18 from receiving [[puberty blockers]], [[hormone therapy]], or [[gender-confirmation surgery|gender-affirming surgery]] beginning in 2024. Doctors who violate the law face up to ten years in prison. It offers no exemptions for minors currently taking puberty blockers or undergoing hormone therapy.<ref name="BSPR Article">{{cite web |last1=Dawson |first1=James |title= Gov. Little signs into law healthcare ban for transgender minors |url= https://www.boisestatepublicradio.org/politics-government/2023-04-04/gov-little-signs-into-law-healthcare-ban-for-transgender-minors |website=Boise State Public Radio |date=5 April 2023 |access-date=29 April 2023}}</ref>

=== Marijuana ===
In a January 2019 interview, Little expressed opposition to legalizing recreational marijuana. He had expressed skepticism about legalizing medical marijuana for patients.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Duke|first=Emily|title=Gov. Little talks medical marijuana, possible routes to legalization|url=https://www.kmvt.com/content/news/Governor-Little-talks-medical-marijuana-possible-routes-to-legalization-504875481.html|access-date=2021-11-01|website=www.kmvt.com|date=25 January 2019 |language=en}}</ref>

When being asked about marijuana legalization in April 2019, Little said: “If Idahoans want legal marijuana, they elected the wrong guy as governor.” [[NORML]], a group advocating the [[Legality of cannabis by U.S. jurisdiction|legalization of marijuana]], gave Little an F rating for his policies about reforming marijuana laws.<ref>{{Cite web|first=Betsy Z.|last=Russell|title=Little: If Idahoans want legal marijuana, 'They elected wrong guy as governor'|url=https://www.idahopress.com/news/local/little-if-idahoans-want-legal-marijuana-they-elected-wrong-guy-as-governor/article_f13c3be4-bd74-58e9-8a6c-92d01ba4f2da.html|access-date=2021-11-02|website=Idaho Press|date=23 April 2019 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Idaho|url=https://norml.org/us-governors/idaho/|access-date=2021-11-02|website=NORML|language=en-US}}</ref>

In February 2021, Little signed Senate Bill 1017, which raises the legal [[Tetrahydrocannabinol|THC]] limit in [[cannabidiol]] (CBD) products from 0% to 0.1% THC. The law went into effect on July 1, 2021.<ref>{{Cite web|title=SENATE BILL 1017 – Idaho State Legislature|url=https://legislature.idaho.gov/sessioninfo/2021/legislation/s1017/|access-date=2021-11-02|language=en-US}}</ref>

In April 2021, Little signed a bill that would legalize the cultivation and transportation of hemp in Idaho with up to 0.3% THC in it, making Idaho the final state to do so, but the bill would prohibit the sale of hemp products containing any THC.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2021-04-19|title=Idaho Gov. signs bill allowing growing, transport of hemp|url=https://apnews.com/article/brad-little-marijuana-idaho-laws-4fb1469a1670d43f70cf12c8b3d68ed5|access-date=2021-11-02|website=AP NEWS|language=en}}</ref>

=== Capital punishment ===

In March 2023, Little, a supporter of [[capital punishment]], signed House Bill 186, which adds a [[firing squad]] as an alternative form of execution when [[lethal injection]] is not available. Idaho is the fifth state to pass such a bill.<ref name="ICS Article">{{cite web |last1=Maldonado |first1=Mia |title= Idaho governor signs bill to allow firing squad as an alternative form of execution |url= https://www.boisestatepublicradio.org/politics-government/2023-04-04/gov-little-signs-into-law-healthcare-ban-for-transgender-minors |website=Idaho Capital Sun |date=5 April 2023 |access-date=29 April 2023}}</ref> In March 2025, he signed a bill making firing squads the state's primary execution method. Idaho became the first state with such a policy.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://idahocapitalsun.com/2025/03/12/idaho-will-be-only-state-with-firing-squad-as-main-execution-method-after-governor-signs-bill/|title=Idaho will be only state with firing squad as main execution method, after governor signs bill|date=March 12, 2025|work=Idaho Capital Sun}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/crime/idaho-governor-bill-firing-squad-b2714148.html|title=Idaho governor signs bill making firing squad the state's primary execution method|date=March 12, 2025|work=}}</ref>

Also in March 2025, Little signed a bill into law that permits the death penalty for rape and sexual abuse of children younger than 12. The law became effective on July 1, 2025. It could ultimately challenge the precedent of ''[[Kennedy v. Louisiana]]''.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://idahocapitalsun.com/2025/03/27/idaho-governor-signs-into-law-child-sex-abuse-death-penalty-bill-despite-u-s-supreme-court-ruling/|title=Idaho governor signs into law child sex abuse death penalty bill, despite U.S. Supreme Court ruling|date=March 27, 2025|work=Idaho Capital Sun}}</ref>

